doc_id: python-architectural-patterns
title: Python Layered Architecture Patterns
project: ai-workflow
version_hint: "2024-2025"
kind: layered_architecture_guide
description: Framework-agnostic layered architecture patterns for separation of concerns, testability, and maintainability across handlers, services, repositories, and contracts/DTOs.
scope: Establishes high-level architectural layers, their responsibilities, allowed call directions, and typical request lifecycle concepts. FastAPI-specific wiring is in docs/development/fastapi/architectural-patterns.toon.
primary_runtime_references[4]:
  - path: app/services/example_service.py
    role: Service layer implementation example.
  - path: app/repositories/example_repository.py
    role: Repository layer implementation example.
  - path: app/contracts/example_contract.py
    role: Contract/DTO layer implementation example.
  - path: app/core/errors.py
    role: Domain error definitions.
related_pattern_docs[4]:
  - fastapi/architectural-patterns.toon
  - python/service-patterns.toon
  - python/repository-patterns.toon
  - python/exception-patterns.toon
sections[5]:
  - id: layer-overview
    index: 1
    title: Layer overview and call directions
    category: architecture
    summary: The architecture enforces strict unidirectional dependencies to ensure testability and separation of concerns.
    items[7]:
      - id: overview.layers
        type: rule
        text: "The application is organized into four primary layers: Handlers/Presentation (HTTP or other transport), Services (business logic), Repositories (data access), and Contracts/DTOs (data structures)."
      - id: overview.call-direction
        type: rule
        text: "Allowed call direction is strictly downward: Handlers -> Services -> Repositories. Upper layers may depend on lower layers, but never the reverse."
      - id: overview.contracts-shared
        type: rule
        text: Contracts/DTOs are shared across all layers for type safety; they define the data shapes passed between layers.
      - id: overview.no-skip-layers
        type: rule
        text: Handlers must not call repositories directly; all data access must go through services to maintain business logic centralization.
      - id: overview.transport-boundary
        type: rule
        text: Only handlers handle transport concerns (HTTP status codes, headers, request/response objects, message queue acknowledgments); services and repositories are transport-agnostic.
      - id: overview.di-flow
        type: rule
        text: Dependencies flow via constructor injection; infrastructure resources (pools, clients) are created at application startup and injected into repositories and services.
      - id: overview.testability
        type: rule
        text: Each layer can be tested in isolation by mocking the layer below; this is the primary benefit of strict layer separation.
  - id: layer-responsibilities
    index: 2
    title: Layer responsibilities
    category: architecture
    summary: Each layer has distinct responsibilities that must not leak into other layers.
    items[8]:
      - id: responsibilities.handlers
        type: rule
        text: "Handlers/Presentation Layer: Handle request/response lifecycle, validate input using framework mechanisms, delegate all processing to services, return response models."
      - id: responsibilities.handlers-no-logic
        type: rule
        text: Handlers contain no business logic; they are thin wrappers that translate transport input to service calls and service output to transport responses.
      - id: responsibilities.services
        type: rule
        text: "Services Layer: Encapsulate business logic, orchestrate repository calls, define transaction boundaries, raise domain-specific exceptions, remain transport-agnostic."
      - id: responsibilities.services-coordination
        type: rule
        text: Services may call multiple repositories and external clients; they own units of work and coordinate complex operations across data stores.
      - id: responsibilities.repositories
        type: rule
        text: "Repositories Layer: Encapsulate data access, hide storage implementation details, return domain models, never commit or roll back transactions."
      - id: responsibilities.repositories-abstraction
        type: rule
        text: Repository protocols (typing.Protocol) enable substitution with fakes or mocks for service unit testing.
      - id: responsibilities.contracts
        type: rule
        text: "Contracts/DTOs Layer: Define data structures and validation rules shared across layers using Pydantic models."
      - id: responsibilities.contracts-validation
        type: rule
        text: "Use strict Pydantic configuration (ConfigDict with extra=\"forbid\") to reject unknown fields and enforce data integrity."
  - id: request-lifecycle
    index: 3
    title: Request lifecycle concepts
    category: architecture
    summary: Describes the generic flow of a request through all architectural layers.
    items[6]:
      - id: lifecycle.step1
        type: note
        text: "1. Request arrives at the application; framework-level processing (validation, authentication) occurs first."
      - id: lifecycle.step2
        type: note
        text: "2. Handler receives validated input and constructs contract DTOs for service method arguments."
      - id: lifecycle.step3
        type: note
        text: "3. Handler calls service method(s), passing validated contract DTOs."
      - id: lifecycle.step4
        type: note
        text: "4. Service orchestrates business logic, calling one or more repositories for data access and coordinating units of work."
      - id: lifecycle.step5
        type: note
        text: "5. Repositories execute queries against data stores (databases, search indices) and return domain models."
      - id: lifecycle.step6
        type: note
        text: "6. Service returns result to handler; handler constructs and returns response model to the framework."
  - id: dependency-flow
    index: 4
    title: Dependency flow and injection principles
    category: architecture
    summary: Dependencies flow downward via constructor injection, enabling testability and explicit composition.
    items[6]:
      - id: di.constructor-injection
        type: rule
        text: Dependencies flow via constructor injection; services receive repositories via constructors, handlers receive services via framework DI mechanisms.
      - id: di.infrastructure
        type: rule
        text: Infrastructure resources (connection pools, HTTP clients) are created at application startup and injected into repositories or services as constructor arguments.
      - id: di.factory-functions
        type: rule
        text: Factory functions compose dependencies, constructing services with their required repositories and configuration.
      - id: di.no-globals
        type: rule
        text: Avoid module-level global state; all dependencies should be explicitly passed through constructors or factory functions.
      - id: di.testability
        type: rule
        text: Each layer can be tested in isolation by mocking the layer below; services mock repositories, handlers mock services.
      - id: di.lifecycle
        type: rule
        text: Resource lifecycle (initialization, cleanup) is managed by the application factory or lifespan manager, not by individual services or repositories.
  - id: anti-patterns
    index: 5
    title: Anti-patterns
    category: anti_patterns
    summary: Common pitfalls that undermine layered architecture design.
    items[6]:
      - id: anti.layer-skipping
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Layer skipping (handlers calling repositories directly) bypasses business logic centralization and makes testing harder.
      - id: anti.logic-in-handlers
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Business logic in handlers violates separation of concerns and makes logic harder to reuse and test.
      - id: anti.transport-in-services
        type: anti_pattern
        text: HTTP or transport concerns in services or repositories couples them to specific frameworks and prevents reuse.
      - id: anti.global-state
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Global state instead of dependency injection hides dependencies and makes testing unreliable.
      - id: anti.mixed-responsibilities
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Mixed responsibilities within a single layer (e.g., services handling HTTP exceptions) violates single responsibility principle.
      - id: anti.upward-dependencies
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Upward dependencies (repositories importing services, services importing handlers) create circular dependencies and coupling.
